[{"title": "Introduction to JavaScript Variables", "content": "Variables in JavaScript are containers for storing data values. In modern JavaScript development, understanding how to manage state and data through variables is the foundation of building interactive web applications. JavaScript is a dynamically typed language, which means you don't need to specify the data type of a variable when you declare it; the engine determines it at runtime based on the value assigned."}, {"title": "1. The 'let' Keyword (ES6+)", "content": "Introduced in ECMAScript 2015 (ES6), 'let' is now the preferred way to declare variables that are expected to change. \n\nKey Characteristics:\n- Block Scoping: Variables declared with 'let' are only available within the block (denoted by curly braces {}) where they are defined.\n- No Redeclaration: You cannot redeclare a 'let' variable in the same scope, preventing accidental overwrites.\n- Reassignment: Unlike 'const', 'let' allows you to update the value later in your code.\n\nExample:\nlet age = 20;\nage = 21; // Valid reassignment"}, {"title": "2. The 'const' Keyword (ES6+)", "content": "The 'const' keyword is used for values that should remain constant throughout the execution of the script.\n\nRules for const:\n- Immutable Binding: You cannot reassign a new value to a constant variable name.\n- Initialization Required: You must assign a value at the moment of declaration.\n- Block Scoped: Like 'let', it respects block boundaries.\n\nImportant Note: While the 'binding' is immutable, the contents of objects or arrays declared with 'const' can still be modified (e.g., adding an element to an array).\n\nExample:\nconst pi = 3.14;\n// pi = 3.15; // Throws TypeError"}, {"title": "3. The 'var' Keyword (Legacy)", "content": "Before ES6, 'var' was the only way to declare variables. Today, it is largely avoided due to its confusing scoping rules.\n\nIssues with var:\n- Function Scoping: 'var' ignores block levels (like 'if' or 'for' loops) and is scoped to the nearest function.\n- Hoisting: Variables declared with 'var' are hoisted to the top of their scope, which can lead to 'undefined' values instead of errors if accessed early.\n- Redeclaration: It allows you to redeclare the same variable name multiple times, leading to bugs."}, {"title": "Primitive Data Types", "content": "JavaScript has 7 primitive data types. Primitives are immutable and passed by value.\n\n1. Number: Represents integers and floating-point numbers.\n2. String: Sequence of characters used for text.\n3. Boolean: Logical values: true or false.\n4. Undefined: A variable that has been declared but not yet assigned a value.\n5. Null: An intentional 'empty' or 'non-existent' value.\n6. BigInt: Used for numbers larger than the Number type can safely represent.\n7. Symbol: A unique and immutable identifier."}, {"title": "Non-Primitive (Reference) Types", "content": "Non-primitive types are objects and are passed by reference.\n\n- Objects: Collections of key-value pairs (e.g., {name: 'Avni', age: 20}).\n- Arrays: Special types of objects used to store ordered lists (e.g., [80, 90, 100]).\n- Functions: Executable blocks of code that can be stored in variables."}, {"title": "Variable Scoping and Lifecycle", "content": "Understanding the lifecycle of a variable is crucial. Global variables exist for as long as the application is running, whereas local variables (inside functions or blocks) are created when the block is entered and destroyed when the block is exited. This 'garbage collection' helps keep the application's memory usage efficient."}, {"title": "Best Practices for Modern JS", "content": "1. Default to 'const': Always use 'const' unless you know the value needs to change.\n2. Use 'let' for counters: Specifically in loops or mathematical increments.\n3. Forget 'var': Treat 'var' as deprecated in modern development environments.\n4. Meaningful Names: Use camelCase for variable names to improve readability."}, {"title": "Deep Dive: Hoisting", "content": "Hoisting is the JavaScript engine's behavior of moving declarations to the top of the current scope. While 'var' is hoisted and initialized as 'undefined', 'let' and 'const' are hoisted but remain in a 'Temporal Dead Zone' (TDZ) until the line of declaration is reached. Accessing them before declaration results in a ReferenceError."}, {"title": "Practical Examples: Arrays and Objects", "content": "const colors = ['red', 'blue'];\ncolors.push('green'); // This works even with const!\n\nlet user = { id: 1, active: true };\nuser.active = false; // Modifying property is allowed."}, {"title": "Comparison Table", "content": "Feature | var | let | const\nScope | Function | Block | Block\nRedeclare | Yes | No | No\nReassign | Yes | Yes | No\nHoisted | Yes (undefined) | Yes (TDZ) | Yes (TDZ)"}, {"title": "The Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)", "content": "The TDZ is the area of a block where a variable is inaccessible until the moment the computer completely initializes it with a value. Using let and const helps identify bugs earlier because the code will crash immediately if you try to use a variable before it is ready."}, {"title": "Memory Management", "content": "JavaScript automatically allocates memory when variables are created and frees it when they are no longer in use. This process is known as garbage collection. By using 'let' and 'const' correctly, you help the engine understand the scope of your data, making memory management more predictable."}, {"title": "Naming Conventions", "content": "Always use descriptive names. Instead of 'let x = 10;', use 'let userAge = 10;'. Use camelCase for variables and functions, and PascalCase for Classes. Constants that are known before execution (like a hex color for a brand) are often written in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., const BRAND_COLOR = '#ff0000')."}, {"title": "Summary and Conclusion", "content": "Mastering variables is the first step toward becoming a proficient JavaScript developer. Use 'const' by default, 'let' when necessary, and 'var' never. Understanding the difference between primitive and reference types will prevent many common bugs related to data mutation."}]